Runs for wiring the per-call planner into the client and streaming response bodies (branch wire, on
main fbd2e6f), 2026-10-29, on the build host: Lean v4.34.1 in localhost/b8perf-lean, uv's CPython
3.14.8 for the servers.  The pure suites ran in ~/lean-http-wire, the real-socket suite, the strace
check and the benchmark in ~/lean-http-wire-net (a second copy, so that no build replaced a binary
mid-run), both on the code of f5bf799.  Main's net-client for the benchmark was built from
`git archive fbd2e6f` in ~/lean-http-wire-main.  The host was shared and loaded (load average
about 150-210 on 128 CPUs).

$ bash logs/2026-10-29/wire/run-final.sh          (from the laptop; the script says what it runs)
$ rsync --archive buildhost:lean-http-wire/out/final/suites logs/2026-10-29/wire/
$ rsync --archive buildhost:lean-http-wire-net/out/final/ logs/2026-10-29/wire/

suites/       run-suites.sh, from a build with .lake/build removed first: build and build-tests (no
              warnings); the axioms of the exchange (proof-axioms), codec, pool and planner
              (call-axioms) theorems, each on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound at most, no
              sorryAx; unit (119 checks: with the planner's record checked against mutated records,
              and the stream's pieces), codec, response-codec, traces, props-seed{1,2}, explore-10,
              diff-seed{1..4} (the same classes and counts as logs/2026-10-29/pool/suites/),
              diff-control, the pool's suites and the planner's call-tests and call-diff.  Every
              file ends with exit status 0.
net/          $ CTR_EXTRA='--sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_syn_retries=2' scripts/remote.sh bash tests/net/run.sh
              291 passed, 0 failed, 1 known limitation (dns-timeout's exit, as in NOTES.md).
              run.log is the console, summary.txt the checks; every call of every scenario was
              checked against the planner (743 calls).
strace-nodelay.txt
              $ ssh buildhost 'cd ~/lean-http-wire-net && bash tests/net/strace-nodelay.sh'
bench/        bench-bigbody.sh (here): a 60 MB body, main's Client.request, this branch's
              Client.request and its withResponse in 64 KiB pulls, 20 randomised blocks (order
              drawn per block, seed 1), server on CPU 100 and client on CPU 101, each cell the median
              of five calls; the binaries' SHA-256 are at the top of bench-bigbody-20.txt.
              paired.txt: $ logs/2026-10-29/wire/bench/paired.py bench/bench-bigbody-20.txt
              request / main 0.98 (95% interval 0.85 to 1.10); stream / main 1.17 (0.95 to 1.23).
reviews/      two rounds of independent review (a fresh Claude and Gemini each), their prompts, and
              README: what came of each finding.

